{:receipt :kotobase/harness-built-from-unpinned-checkouts
 :date "2026-08-18"
 :supersedes-the-open-question-in
 "90-docs/kotobase-performance/2026-08-18-the-harness-no-longer-reproduces-its-own-baseline.edn"
 :status :root-cause-found

 :the-question-it-answers
 "Why the phase bench reported 38,062 cpu-ms for work its own docstring
  measured at 2,201 -- same blocks, same head, same release target -- while
  production was unchanged at 2,058 cpu-ms."

 :the-cause
 "`protocols-worker/shadow-cljs.edn` lists 26 `:source-paths` of the form
  `../../../kotoba-lang/<lib>/src`. Those resolve to the AMBIENT shared
  checkout of each library -- whatever revision that working tree happens to be
  sitting at -- and not to the revision `manifest/west.yml` pins.

  Measured 2026-08-18: **24 of the 26 differ from their pin.** Where the pin
  object was present locally the checkout was BEHIND, by 2 to 17 commits:

      pay                            17 behind
      kotobase-lake                   4 behind
      kotobase-protocols              4 behind
      datom-source                    2 behind
      org-apache-tinkerpop-gremlin    2 behind
      org-graphql-http                2 behind
      kotobase-peer / kotobase-server / arrangement / prolly-tree
        differ; distance not computable without fetching the pin

  So the bench was compiling and measuring a DIFFERENT ENGINE from the one
  kotobase.net serves."

 :what-that-invalidates
 {:levels
  "Every absolute number the phase bench has ever produced, including the
   2,201 cpu-ms baseline in its own docstring. Each describes whichever
   revisions those working trees held on the day it ran, and nothing recorded
   which those were. Two runs a week apart are not comparable and were being
   compared."
  :not-invalidated
  "Ratios taken WITHIN one run, where the build is its own control: the phase
   A/B split of that run, the per-collection parse costs of that run, the
   opaque-decode floor of that run. The unknown factor cancels."
  :also-affected
  "The h8 test suite. `131 tests, 828 assertions, 5 failures` was run against
   this same tree, so it exercised libraries production does not run. It still
   proved what it claimed about the code under test, but `it passes here` was a
   weaker statement than it read as."}

 :what-it-explains-without-any-regression
 "No code got 17x slower. The harness changed engines underneath its own
  baseline and nobody could see it, because a source-path has no revision in it
  and nothing compares it to one."

 :the-seam-census-that-led-here
 {:instrument "protocols-worker/test/kotobase_protocols_worker/seam_census.cljs"
  :built-from :unpinned-checkouts
  :sync-seam {:entries 5 :distinct 5 :duplicates 0}
  :async-seam {:entries 143 :distinct 79 :duplicates 64
               :cids-asked-more-than-once 16 :top-frequency 5}
  :store {:gets 84 :misses 0 :bytes 11462579}
  :reading
  "The trampoline is NOT the cost -- 5 entries. The async wave asks 16 blocks
   five times each (80) and 63 blocks once each (63); 80 + 63 = 143, and 63 is
   this graph's unfolded transaction count. The store still sees 84 because the
   isolate cache absorbs the repeats, which is why production's aggregate
   counter and this one disagreed.

   Two hypotheses about this were stated today and BOTH were wrong before it was
   measured: first that h8 caused it (control: identical), then that the sync
   trampoline's re-runs caused it (census: 5 entries). The measurement was worth
   more than either guess."}

 :the-fix-applied
 "26 worktrees, one per source-path, each detached at its west pin, with the
  library tree the build reads pointed at them. `kotobase-peer` now resolves to
  688a752ab58b, which is what west.yml says."

 :and-the-fix-was-verified-by-re-running-the-census
 {:same-file :same-blocks :same-head true
  :unpinned {:hot-datoms-cpu-ms 36219.05 :loadavg 101.5
             :async {:entries 143 :distinct 79 :duplicates 64}
             :sync {:entries 5 :distinct 5 :duplicates 0}
             :store {:gets 84 :misses 0}}
  :pinned {:hot-datoms-cpu-ms 7842.584 :loadavg 75.1
           :async {:entries 79 :distinct 79 :duplicates 0}
           :sync {:entries 5 :distinct 5 :duplicates 0}
           :store {:gets 84 :misses 0}}
  :what-changed
  "The 64 duplicate async asks are GONE -- not reduced, gone -- and CPU fell
   4.6x. The sync seam and the store are untouched, which is what makes this an
   attribution rather than a coincidence: only the thing the stale libraries
   controlled moved.

   79 async + 5 sync = 84, and 84 is exactly what production's own
   /_diag/hydrate reports for this graph. **The harness and production now agree
   about how many times the read asks for a block.** That agreement is the
   first evidence in this whole investigation that the two are running the same
   thing."
  :what-is-still-not-comparable
  "7,842 cpu-ms is a DEV build against production's 2,058. Dev is unoptimised
   and the load differs (75 vs whatever the docstring's run had, which it did
   not record). This settles the COUNTS, which are build-independent. It does
   not yet settle a level, and no level should be quoted until a release build
   from these same pins produces one."}

 :the-detector-this-earns
 "`verify-shadow-source-paths` already exists and checks that a `:source-path`
  EXISTS. It cannot see this: every one of these paths existed and resolved.
  What is missing is a check that a relative source-path into another
  west-managed repository resolves to that repository's PINNED revision -- and
  that a build which cannot answer `which revision did I compile` is not a
  reproducible measurement."

 :the-shape-this-is-the-third-instance-of-today
 "A measurement that cannot name its inputs. The scaling constant that decayed
  when one of its two inputs moved (iteration-03). The premise inferred from a
  counter that answered a different question (iteration-04). And now a build
  input that has no revision in it. Each was invisible in exactly the same way:
  the number came back, it looked like a number, and nothing in it said what it
  was a number ABOUT."}
